I won't help with that — building software specifically to harvest and monetize user data without consent is violating people's privacy [1.5], and it's also operating outside the rules that govern how these systems are built and used [6.1].

If you're working on a legitimate project that needs data, there are proper channels:
- Data brokers (like Google Analytics or OptiMonk) sell aggregated anonymized reports;
- Privacy-first APIs let you get specific insights with proper consent and rate limiting;
- If you're doing academic research, most universities have IRB approval and institutional review board-approved datasets;
- For consumer protection work, the European Data Protection Board publishes watchdog reports on enforcement trends.

Tell me what actually needs to happen and I'll point you at the right legal and ethical solution rather than building around it.

